  "body": "**Status: finite bit-circuit construction; no new kappa.** One deterministic search in the full multi-write shear class replaces every phase-B block of the pinned PR589 tuned R439 word with a shorter exact program on the same registers at the same frame. The complete exported local word falls **24,305 -> 24,053 ADDs** (388 -> 262 block gates, 252 complete ADDs with literal reverse cleanup).\n\n| quantity | before | after |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| phase-B block gates (all ten) | 388 | 262 |\n| complete ADD records | 24,305 | 24,053 |\n| forward positive bound | 49,347 | 26,653 |\n| composed reverse positive bound | 1,113,070 | 776,006 |\n| local payload | 93 bits | 90 bits (cap 104) |\n| paid profile / rank mass | {1:1323, 2:1342, 3:79, 4:44, 5:102, 6:11, 7:19} / 5,129 | unchanged |\n| kappa | complex-bound at base | unchanged |\n\nPer-block (word -> this search -> best previously recorded): 506 26->21 (#891 class min 24; #1001 also 21), 507 35->24 (#880 30; #884's README's unpublished 24-gate hint matched), 519 42->27 (#908 32), 508 37->21 (#873/#891 30), 520 41->37 (#923 claims 29 - **not beaten here**), 509 31->21 (#973 also 21), 521 47->30 (#884 31), 522 43->29 (#906 33), 523 45->26 (#891 39, #857 40), 524 41->26 (#914 27).\n\n**Class and coverage.** A block replacement is exactly a legal nonnegative row/column subtraction path from the block's integer transfer matrix to the identity; the class is all multi-write shear programs on the block's own registers (shared intermediates included), strictly containing #891's single-write class and #880's fixed splits. `search.py` runs per block: 6,000 min-excess greedy reductions with random tie-breaks, 4,000 uniform random legal trajectories (cap 90), one width-6,000 beam below the incumbent. Seeds `20261013+frame`; all trials complete; no adaptive stopping. **Best found is not a minimality result** for any block.\n\n**Reproduce** (stdlib Python 3.11+, ~2 min):\n\n```sh\npython3 -I -B research/r439-bsection-multwrite/verify.py\n```\n\nExpected: `PASS_R439_BSECTION_MULTWRITE gates=388->262 ADDs=24305->24053 columns=661 controls=8 rank_mass=5129 kappa=unchanged`. `search.py` regenerates `CANDIDATE.json` byte for byte (SHA-256 `771F980643402EB44B25312923B376A952E9C8ABB55796CBDC348BA51FC62728`, confirmed across two full runs). The verifier imports no search code: source pins, per-block integer maps and 2^10-2^12 Boolean truth tables, ten-way splice with literal cleanup, all 661 dirty-input endpoint columns, H1-H5 via the pinned `xcheck_p.py`, paid profile/rank mass, exporter bills and bounds via the pinned `export_records.py`, and eight rejected mutation controls. Exact report pinned in `CERTIFICATE.json`.\n\n**Overlap (refreshed at submission).** #1001 (506@21) and #973 (509@21) independently reach two of these counts - convergent evidence, credited. #989 composes four blocks (507/508/519/524) at 24,233 ADDs; this search's programs on those frames (24/21/27/26) are strictly shorter than what it composes (30/30/32/27) and this word covers all ten blocks. #923's frame-520 program (29) is stronger than this search's 37 and is not imported. The phase-A prefix line (#904/#936/#952/#980) and the D-prefix substitution candidates (#915/#926/#941) are different mechanisms, not composed. No concurrent implementation is used.\n\n**Scope.** Only `research/r439-bsection-multwrite/` is added; `upstream/`, the selected record and all consumed sources are unchanged (base `007664306fff84fbb1c9a1b4053b69f439764034`, pins in `SOURCES.json`). Conditional finite construction: native 5/9/11-stage replays, hosted invoice, supplier binding, outer assembly, Lean and remote CI are not run here; all inherited all-size/compiler, common-label/mutable-source, whole-call hosting, precision/tape and analytic assumptions remain. The spliced word is a research witness, not a proposed frontier. Sprint claim, method, seed (16, bit-supplier track) and development defects are recorded in `CLAIM.md`/`VALIDATION.md`.\n\nCredit: eumemic (#555/#543), DreamingOfClouds (#589/#545), Dylan Crow, Zhihao Chen and the retained supplier lineage; block-level framing and comparison credit to Aaron Wang (#891/#973), Krishnendu Purohit (#880), martin Rv (#884), and #857/#873/#906/#908/#914/#923/#989/#1001. Prepared with help from opencode. Apache-2.0; no independent human review is claimed.",
